Why Is Demand Increasing
Technology migration to hybrid bonding. Besi reported Q2 2026 revenue growth of 68.7% year over year, citing broad-based demand led by hybrid bonding, photonics and data-center applications. Semiconductor Insight rates technology migration a high-impact driver because hybrid bonding and fine-pitch thermocompression increase product value and complexity.
New use cases beyond logic. On 18 June 2026, Besi raised its long-term financial targets at its 2026 Investor Day, citing new hybrid-bonding use cases in logic, memory and co-packaged optics. Semiconductor Insight also rates electronics demand a high-impact driver, as growth in automotive, data centers, industrial automation and connected devices expands the installed base that requires bonders.
Finer interconnect pitch in AI hardware. Leading assembly-equipment suppliers continue to increase support for thermocompression and hybrid bonding as AI accelerators, HBM and chiplets move to finer interconnect pitch, lifting the market from USD 2.44 billion in 2025 to an estimated USD 2.55 billion in 2026. Supply diversification, rated medium-high impact, adds further pull as customers seek qualified capacity across several regions.
Fab & Technology Pulse: Four Ways to Join a Die

The Bonder market is no longer one equipment category. It is four bonding technologies serving different packaging problems, and the value is moving toward the finest pitch. Thermocompression handles fine-pitch die-to-die and die-to-wafer bonding for advanced packaging. Hybrid bonding forms direct dielectric and copper connections for very fine interconnect pitch, which is why Semiconductor Insight identifies it, together with fine-pitch thermocompression, as a central competitive differentiator. Ultrasonic and wedge bonding remains the workhorse for wire bonding and interconnect in mature and power packages, and eutectic or adhesive bonding covers die attach where thermal and mechanical requirements are specialized. The commercial consequence: buyers weigh qualification history, integration effort and lifecycle support alongside price, and qualification can take many months. That favors suppliers who can prove process control at smaller geometries rather than those competing on tool price alone.

Segmentation Highlights
By type: Wire Bonder, Die Bonder and Flip Chip Bonder, with Wire Bonder identified as a leading segment. Higher-value variants offer tighter tolerances and greater integration, while mainstream categories keep volume leadership through lower cost and broad compatibility.
By bonding technology: Thermocompression, Hybrid Bonding, Ultrasonic/Wedge, and Eutectic/Adhesive.
By application: Consumer Electronics, Automotive Electronics, Telecommunications, Industrial Manufacturing and Medical Devices, with Consumer Electronics a major application. Premium applications require stricter qualification and long-term supply support, while higher-volume applications emphasize cost, manufacturing scale and rapid technology migration.
By end user: IDM, OSAT, Foundry/Advanced Packaging (2.5D, 3D and chiplet integration) and Optoelectronics (photonics, sensors and compound-semiconductor package assembly).
Regional Outlook
Asia Pacific: The largest regional market and the strongest growth profile. China, Taiwan, South Korea, Japan and Southeast Asia hold the largest concentration of OSAT, memory, logic and advanced packaging capacity.
North America: A high-value market supported by U.S. technology companies, data centers, aerospace, automotive and advanced manufacturing, with the United States leading on technology development, premium applications and design leadership, and Canada and Mexico contributing manufacturing and regional integration.
Europe: Strategically important through automotive, industrial, telecom and aerospace applications, where buyers emphasize reliability, environmental compliance and long product lifecycles.
South America and Middle East & Africa: Emerging markets. Brazil leads South America, while Gulf investment in digital infrastructure creates selective opportunities in the Middle East.

